---
import ArticleLayout from "@layouts/ArticleLayout.astro";
---

<ArticleLayout
  title="Bus 351"
  description="An n8n automation that checks Transport for NSW for the next route 351 departure and sends a plain-language delay message to WhatsApp."
  subtitle="Is the bus late? A message answers before I get to the stop."
  eyebrow="2026 · Automation / n8n"
  tags="Automation · n8n · Transport for NSW · WhatsApp"
>
  <p class="lead">
    A workflow checks the live departure board for route 351 a few times each
    weekday, compares the estimated time with the timetable, and sends one
    sentence to WhatsApp. On schedule, running late, running early, or not
    tracked yet.
  </p>

  <figure class="workflow-shot">
    <a href="/assets/bus-351/workflow.png">
      <img
        src="/assets/bus-351/workflow.png"
        alt="The n8n canvas showing five connected nodes: Schedule, Select stop, Get departures, Format message and Send text"
        width="1265"
        height="523"
        loading="lazy"
      />
    </a>
    <figcaption>
      The workflow: a weekday trigger, a stop chosen by local time, the
      departure board, the message logic and a WhatsApp send. Open the full
      screenshot.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <section>
    <h2>A message, not a departure board</h2>
    <p>
      The trip planning API returns a full board: every route at the stop, with
      planned and estimated times. Reading it on a phone while walking is a poor
      experience. The workflow reduces all of it to the one decision I actually
      make — leave now, or wait.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>The steps</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <strong>Schedule</strong> — three cron expressions on weekdays: every 10
        minutes from 8:00, 14:50, then every 10 minutes from 15:00.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Select stop</strong> — a Code node reads the hour in
        <code>Australia/Sydney</code> and picks a stop ID. Morning runs use one
        stop, afternoon runs use another. This matters because the schedule
        events fire in server time, not local time.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Get departures</strong> — calls the Transport for NSW
        <code>departure_mon</code> endpoint with <code>rapidJSON</code> output
        and an API key header.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Format message</strong> — filters <code>stopEvents</code> to
        route <code>351</code>, drops anything that has already left, sorts by
        estimated time and compares it with the planned time.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Send text</strong> — posts the sentence to a WhatsApp group
        through the Evolution API node.
      </li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>The message the code produces</h2>
    <p>
      If there is live tracking, the difference between planned and estimated
      becomes plain language. A difference of zero is{" "}
      <em>“Bus 351 is on schedule.”</em> A positive difference reports how many
      minutes late it is, a negative one how many minutes early. Without
      tracking, the message falls back to the scheduled time so the silence is
      not mistaken for a delay.
    </p>
    <p>
      No departures in the window produces{" "}
      <em>“Bus 351: no upcoming departures found.”</em> Matching on the route
      number, rather than the first result in the board, is what keeps a
      neighbouring route out of the message.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What is still fragile</h2>
    <p>
      Buses that left in the last two minutes are kept, so a slightly stale
      departure does not vanish mid-check. Code and time zone logic live inside
      the workflow rather than next to the rest of the codebase, so there are no
      tests around the format step. The stop IDs are also hard-coded; replacing
      them with a lookup by stop name would make the workflow portable.
    </p>
  </section>

  <aside class="project-status">
    Working automation · The Transport for NSW API key, the WhatsApp instance
    and the group ID are configured in n8n credentials and are not published
    here.
  </aside>
